{
 "id": "number-theory",
 "title": "Number Theory",
 "code": "NT",
 "draft": 1,
 "track": "math",
 "requires": [
  "discrete-mathematics"
 ],
 "level": 3,
 "domains": [
  "nt"
 ],
 "basis": {
  "primary": "cambridge-ii-number-theory",
  "also": [
   "cambridge-ia-numbers-sets"
  ],
  "note": "Anchored on the Cambridge Numbers and Sets and Number Theory schedules (read through an extraction). The Jacobi symbol, binary quadratic forms and factorisation methods (T4, T5, T9) are not drilled in draft 1."
 },
 "big_ideas": [
  {
   "id": "MOD",
   "title": "Work in the right modulus",
   "text": "Most questions about integers become finite once reduced modulo the right number; the art is choosing it."
  },
  {
   "id": "STRUCT",
   "title": "The units modulo n form a group",
   "text": "Fermat, Euler, Wilson and primitive roots are all statements about the group of units, and its order is φ(n)."
  }
 ],
 "practices": [
  {
   "id": "P1",
   "title": "Compute",
   "text": "Carry the definition or theorem to a number, a count or an example."
  },
  {
   "id": "P2",
   "title": "Decide",
   "text": "Say whether a statement holds, which hypothesis fails, or which example breaks it."
  }
 ],
 "units": [
  {
   "id": "divisibility",
   "n": 1,
   "title": "Divisibility and factorisation",
   "std": [
    "cambridge-ii-number-theory:T1",
    "cambridge-ia-numbers-sets:N4"
   ],
   "scope": "core",
   "ideas": [
    "MOD"
   ],
   "topics": [
    {
     "n": 1,
     "title": "Euclid and unique factorisation",
     "los": [
      {
       "id": "A",
       "std": [
        "cambridge-ii-number-theory:T1",
        "cambridge-ia-numbers-sets:N4"
       ],
       "do": "Run Euclid's algorithm and say how long it takes.",
       "ek": [
        "gcd(a, b) = gcd(b, a mod b); the last non-zero remainder is the gcd. Consecutive Fibonacci numbers are the slowest inputs."
       ],
       "p": [
        "P1"
       ],
       "needs": [
        "DM-1.2.A",
        "NT-1.2.C"
       ],
       "drills": [
        "nt-gcd",
        "nt-euclid-steps"
       ]
      },
      {
       "id": "B",
       "std": [
        "cambridge-ii-number-theory:T1",
        "cambridge-ia-numbers-sets:N4"
       ],
       "do": "Solve a linear congruence or a linear Diophantine equation with Bezout's identity.",
       "ek": [
        "ax + by = c has integer solutions exactly when gcd(a, b) divides c; running Euclid backwards finds one, and the rest differ by multiples of (b/g, −a/g)."
       ],
       "p": [
        "P1"
       ],
       "needs": [
        "NT-1.1.A"
       ],
       "drills": [
        "nt-linear-congruence",
        "nt-coin-solutions"
       ]
      },
      {
       "id": "C",
       "std": [
        "cambridge-ii-number-theory:T1",
        "cambridge-ia-numbers-sets:N4"
       ],
       "do": "Count and sum the divisors of an integer from its prime factorisation.",
       "ek": [
        "If n = Π p^a then n has Π (a + 1) divisors and σ(n) = Π (p^(a+1) − 1)/(p − 1); both functions are multiplicative."
       ],
       "p": [
        "P1"
       ],
       "needs": [
        "NT-1.1.A",
        "NT-1.2.C",
        "NT-1.2.B"
       ],
       "drills": [
        "nt-divisor-count",
        "nt-divisor-sum"
       ]
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "n": 2,
     "title": "Remainders and factorisations",
     "los": [
      {
       "id": "A",
       "std": [
        "cambridge-ii-number-theory:T1",
        "cambridge-ia-numbers-sets:N4"
       ],
       "do": "Apply the division algorithm, including to negative numbers.",
       "ek": [
        "For any integer a and positive n there is exactly one pair q, r with a = qn + r and 0 ≤ r < n. The remainder is never negative: −7 = (−2)·5 + 3."
       ],
       "p": [
        "P1"
       ],
       "needs": [
        "DM-1.2.A"
       ],
       "drills": [
        "nt-remainder-negative",
        "nt-quotient-remainder"
       ]
      },
      {
       "id": "B",
       "std": [
        "cambridge-ii-number-theory:T1",
        "cambridge-ia-numbers-sets:N4"
       ],
       "do": "Find how often a prime divides a factorial.",
       "ek": [
        "The exponent of p in n! is ⌊n/p⌋ + ⌊n/p²⌋ + ⌊n/p³⌋ + … (Legendre). The number of trailing zeros of n! is the exponent of 5."
       ],
       "p": [
        "P1"
       ],
       "needs": [
        "NT-1.2.A"
       ],
       "drills": [
        "nt-prime-in-factorial",
        "nt-trailing-zeros"
       ]
      },
      {
       "id": "C",
       "std": [
        "cambridge-ii-number-theory:T1",
        "cambridge-ia-numbers-sets:N4"
       ],
       "do": "Read the gcd and lcm off prime factorisations.",
       "ek": [
        "Take the smaller exponent of each prime for the gcd and the larger for the lcm, so gcd(a, b) · lcm(a, b) = ab."
       ],
       "p": [
        "P1"
       ],
       "needs": [
        "NT-1.2.A"
       ],
       "drills": [
        "nt-lcm-from-factors",
        "nt-gcd-lcm-product"
       ]
      }
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "congruences",
   "n": 2,
   "title": "Congruences",
   "std": [
    "cambridge-ii-number-theory:T1",
    "cambridge-ii-number-theory:T2",
    "cambridge-ia-numbers-sets:N4"
   ],
   "scope": "core",
   "ideas": [
    "MOD",
    "STRUCT"
   ],
   "topics": [
    {
     "n": 1,
     "title": "Arithmetic modulo n",
     "los": [
      {
       "id": "A",
       "std": [
        "cambridge-ii-number-theory:T1",
        "cambridge-ia-numbers-sets:N4"
       ],
       "do": "Find a modular inverse and reduce a large power with Fermat's little theorem.",
       "ek": [
        "a has an inverse modulo n exactly when gcd(a, n) = 1. For a prime p not dividing a, a^(p−1) ≡ 1 (mod p), so exponents reduce modulo p − 1."
       ],
       "p": [
        "P1"
       ],
       "needs": [
        "NT-1.1.B",
        "NT-2.2.C"
       ],
       "drills": [
        "nt-inverse",
        "nt-fermat-power"
       ]
      },
      {
       "id": "B",
       "std": [
        "cambridge-ii-number-theory:T1",
        "cambridge-ia-numbers-sets:N4"
       ],
       "do": "Compute Euler's function and use Euler's theorem.",
       "ek": [
        "φ(n) = n Π (1 − 1/p) over the primes dividing n, and a^φ(n) ≡ 1 (mod n) whenever gcd(a, n) = 1."
       ],
       "p": [
        "P1"
       ],
       "needs": [
        "NT-2.1.A",
        "NT-1.1.C"
       ],
       "drills": [
        "nt-euler-phi",
        "nt-last-two-digits"
       ]
      },
      {
       "id": "C",
       "std": [
        "cambridge-ii-number-theory:T2",
        "cambridge-ia-numbers-sets:N4"
       ],
       "do": "Solve simultaneous congruences and use Wilson's theorem.",
       "ek": [
        "If m and n are coprime, x ≡ a (mod m), x ≡ b (mod n) has exactly one solution modulo mn. Wilson: (p − 1)! ≡ −1 (mod p) exactly when p is prime."
       ],
       "p": [
        "P1"
       ],
       "needs": [
        "NT-2.1.A"
       ],
       "drills": [
        "nt-crt",
        "nt-wilson"
       ]
      },
      {
       "id": "D",
       "std": [
        "cambridge-ia-numbers-sets:N4"
       ],
       "do": "Find an RSA decryption exponent and encrypt a small message.",
       "ek": [
        "With n = pq and φ = (p − 1)(q − 1), the public exponent e and private exponent d satisfy ed ≡ 1 (mod φ); encryption is m^e mod n and decryption c^d mod n."
       ],
       "p": [
        "P1"
       ],
       "needs": [
        "NT-2.1.B",
        "NT-2.2.D"
       ],
       "drills": [
        "nt-rsa-private",
        "nt-rsa-encrypt"
       ]
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "n": 2,
     "title": "Congruence arithmetic",
     "los": [
      {
       "id": "A",
       "std": [
        "cambridge-ii-number-theory:T1",
        "cambridge-ia-numbers-sets:N4"
       ],
       "do": "Add, multiply and take powers of residues.",
       "ek": [
        "Congruence respects addition and multiplication, so any integer may be replaced by its remainder at any stage. Powers of a residue repeat in a cycle."
       ],
       "p": [
        "P1"
       ],
       "needs": [
        "NT-1.2.A"
       ],
       "drills": [
        "nt-residue-expression",
        "nt-last-digit-power"
       ]
      },
      {
       "id": "B",
       "std": [
        "cambridge-ii-number-theory:T1",
        "cambridge-ia-numbers-sets:N4"
       ],
       "do": "Explain and use the digit tests for 9 and 11.",
       "ek": [
        "Since 10 ≡ 1 (mod 9), a number is congruent to its digit sum modulo 9; since 10 ≡ −1 (mod 11), it is congruent to its alternating digit sum modulo 11."
       ],
       "p": [
        "P1"
       ],
       "needs": [
        "NT-2.2.A"
       ],
       "drills": [
        "nt-mod-nine",
        "nt-mod-eleven"
       ]
      },
      {
       "id": "C",
       "std": [
        "cambridge-ii-number-theory:T1",
        "cambridge-ia-numbers-sets:N4"
       ],
       "do": "Solve a linear congruence and count its solutions.",
       "ek": [
        "ax ≡ b (mod n) has a solution exactly when d = gcd(a, n) divides b, and then it has exactly d solutions modulo n."
       ],
       "p": [
        "P1"
       ],
       "needs": [
        "NT-2.2.A",
        "NT-1.1.B"
       ],
       "drills": [
        "nt-congruence-count",
        "nt-congruence-solve"
       ]
      },
      {
       "id": "D",
       "std": [
        "cambridge-ii-number-theory:T1",
        "cambridge-ia-numbers-sets:N4"
       ],
       "do": "Reduce an exponent with Euler's theorem and invert with Fermat's.",
       "ek": [
        "If gcd(a, n) = 1 then exponents may be reduced modulo φ(n). Modulo a prime p, the inverse of a is a^(p−2)."
       ],
       "p": [
        "P1"
       ],
       "needs": [
        "NT-2.1.A",
        "NT-2.1.B"
       ],
       "drills": [
        "nt-euler-reduce",
        "nt-fermat-inverse"
       ]
      }
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "residues",
   "n": 3,
   "title": "Primitive roots and quadratic residues",
   "std": [
    "cambridge-ii-number-theory:T2",
    "cambridge-ii-number-theory:T3"
   ],
   "scope": "core",
   "ideas": [
    "STRUCT"
   ],
   "topics": [
    {
     "n": 1,
     "title": "The structure of the units",
     "los": [
      {
       "id": "A",
       "std": [
        "cambridge-ii-number-theory:T2"
       ],
       "do": "Find the order of an element modulo a prime and count primitive roots.",
       "ek": [
        "The order of a modulo p divides p − 1. A primitive root has order p − 1; every prime has φ(p − 1) of them."
       ],
       "p": [
        "P1"
       ],
       "needs": [
        "NT-2.1.B"
       ],
       "drills": [
        "nt-order-mod-p",
        "nt-primitive-root-count"
       ]
      },
      {
       "id": "B",
       "std": [
        "cambridge-ii-number-theory:T3"
       ],
       "do": "Evaluate a Legendre symbol with Euler's criterion and the reciprocity laws.",
       "ek": [
        "(a/p) ≡ a^((p−1)/2) (mod p). (−1/p) = 1 exactly when p ≡ 1 (mod 4); (2/p) = 1 exactly when p ≡ ±1 (mod 8); for odd primes (p/q)(q/p) = −1 only when both are 3 mod 4."
       ],
       "p": [
        "P1"
       ],
       "needs": [
        "NT-3.1.A",
        "NT-3.2.B"
       ],
       "drills": [
        "nt-legendre",
        "nt-reciprocity-facts"
       ]
      },
      {
       "id": "C",
       "std": [
        "cambridge-ii-number-theory:T4"
       ],
       "do": "Evaluate a Jacobi symbol by reciprocity and say what it does and does not tell you.",
       "ek": [
        "The Jacobi symbol (a/n) for odd n multiplies the Legendre symbols over the prime factors of n and obeys the same reciprocity rules, so it is computed without factorising. (a/n) = −1 means a is not a square modulo n; (a/n) = 1 does not mean it is."
       ],
       "p": [
        "P1"
       ],
       "needs": [
        "NT-3.1.B"
       ],
       "drills": [
        "nt-jacobi",
        "nt-jacobi-facts"
       ]
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "n": 2,
     "title": "Squares modulo a prime",
     "los": [
      {
       "id": "A",
       "std": [
        "cambridge-ii-number-theory:T3"
       ],
       "do": "List the squares modulo a prime and count the solutions of x² ≡ a.",
       "ek": [
        "Modulo an odd prime p the non-zero squares are the (p − 1)/2 values of 1², 2², …, ((p − 1)/2)². x² ≡ a has two solutions if a is a non-zero square, one if a ≡ 0, none otherwise."
       ],
       "p": [
        "P1"
       ],
       "needs": [
        "NT-2.2.A"
       ],
       "drills": [
        "nt-square-solutions",
        "nt-count-squares"
       ]
      },
      {
       "id": "B",
       "std": [
        "cambridge-ii-number-theory:T3"
       ],
       "do": "Apply Euler's criterion by direct computation.",
       "ek": [
        "a^((p−1)/2) is congruent to 1 if a is a non-zero square modulo p and to −1 (that is, p − 1) if it is not."
       ],
       "p": [
        "P1"
       ],
       "needs": [
        "NT-3.2.A",
        "NT-2.1.A"
       ],
       "drills": [
        "nt-euler-criterion",
        "nt-criterion-facts"
       ]
      }
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "diophantine",
   "n": 4,
   "title": "Diophantine equations and primes",
   "std": [
    "cambridge-ii-number-theory:T6",
    "cambridge-ii-number-theory:T7",
    "cambridge-ii-number-theory:T8"
   ],
   "scope": "core",
   "ideas": [
    "MOD"
   ],
   "topics": [
    {
     "n": 1,
     "title": "Continued fractions, squares and primality",
     "los": [
      {
       "id": "B",
       "std": [
        "cambridge-ii-number-theory:T3",
        "cambridge-ib-grm:RI"
       ],
       "do": "Decide whether an integer is a sum of two squares.",
       "ek": [
        "An odd prime is a sum of two squares exactly when it is 1 mod 4. In general n is one exactly when every prime that is 3 mod 4 divides n to an even power."
       ],
       "p": [
        "P2"
       ],
       "needs": [
        "NT-3.1.B",
        "NT-4.2.C"
       ],
       "drills": [
        "nt-sum-two-squares",
        "nt-two-squares-facts"
       ]
      },
      {
       "id": "A",
       "std": [
        "cambridge-ii-number-theory:T7"
       ],
       "do": "Expand a rational as a continued fraction and solve a Pell equation.",
       "ek": [
        "The continued fraction of p/q is Euclid's algorithm written down. The convergents of √d give the solutions of x² − dy² = 1, and every solution is a power of the smallest."
       ],
       "p": [
        "P1"
       ],
       "needs": [
        "NT-1.1.A"
       ],
       "drills": [
        "nt-continued-fraction",
        "nt-pell"
       ]
      },
      {
       "id": "C",
       "std": [
        "cambridge-ii-number-theory:T6",
        "cambridge-ii-number-theory:T8"
       ],
       "do": "Estimate how many primes lie below a bound and apply the Fermat primality test.",
       "ek": [
        "π(x) is about x/ln x. If a^(n−1) is not 1 modulo n then n is composite; composites that pass for base a are pseudoprimes, and Carmichael numbers such as 561 pass for every coprime base."
       ],
       "p": [
        "P1"
       ],
       "needs": [
        "NT-2.1.A",
        "NT-4.2.A"
       ],
       "drills": [
        "nt-prime-count-estimate",
        "nt-fermat-test"
       ]
      },
      {
       "id": "D",
       "std": [
        "cambridge-ii-number-theory:T9"
       ],
       "do": "Factorise an integer by writing it as a difference of squares.",
       "ek": [
        "If n = a² − b² then n = (a − b)(a + b). Fermat's method tries a = ⌈√n⌉, ⌈√n⌉ + 1, … until a² − n is a square. More generally x² ≡ y² (mod n) with x ≢ ±y gives the factor gcd(x − y, n)."
       ],
       "p": [
        "P1"
       ],
       "needs": [
        "NT-1.1.C"
       ],
       "drills": [
        "nt-fermat-factorise",
        "nt-congruent-squares"
       ]
      }
     ]
    },
    {
     "n": 2,
     "title": "Primes up close",
     "los": [
      {
       "id": "A",
       "std": [
        "cambridge-ii-number-theory:T6"
       ],
       "do": "Count primes in a range with the sieve of Eratosthenes.",
       "ek": [
        "To find the primes up to N, cross out multiples of each prime up to √N; what survives is prime. A number below N with no prime factor up to √N is prime."
       ],
       "p": [
        "P1"
       ],
       "needs": [
        "NT-1.2.A"
       ],
       "drills": [
        "nt-count-primes",
        "nt-trial-division-bound"
       ]
      },
      {
       "id": "B",
       "std": [
        "cambridge-ii-number-theory:T1",
        "cambridge-ii-number-theory:T8"
       ],
       "do": "Connect Mersenne primes to even perfect numbers.",
       "ek": [
        "If 2ᵖ − 1 is prime then 2ᵖ⁻¹(2ᵖ − 1) is perfect, and every even perfect number has this form (Euclid, Euler). 2ᵖ − 1 can only be prime when p is prime, but need not be: 2¹¹ − 1 = 23 · 89."
       ],
       "p": [
        "P1"
       ],
       "needs": [
        "NT-1.1.C",
        "NT-4.2.A"
       ],
       "drills": [
        "nt-perfect-from-mersenne",
        "nt-mersenne-facts"
       ]
      },
      {
       "id": "C",
       "std": [
        "cambridge-ii-number-theory:T7"
       ],
       "do": "Generate Pythagorean triples.",
       "ek": [
        "For m > n > 0, (m² − n², 2mn, m² + n²) is a Pythagorean triple; it is primitive exactly when m and n are coprime and of opposite parity, and every primitive triple arises this way."
       ],
       "p": [
        "P1"
       ],
       "needs": [
        "NT-1.2.C"
       ],
       "drills": [
        "nt-triple-hypotenuse",
        "nt-triple-primitive"
       ]
      }
     ]
    }
   ]
  }
